WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.730
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let's talk about AWS KMS,</v>

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.680
which is the key management service.

3
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.477
So anytime you hear encryption in AWS,

4
00:00:06.477 --> 00:00:08.880
you have to think about KMS, obviously,

5
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.610
and it's a way to easily control access to our data,

6
00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.160
and AWS will manage the encryption keys for us.

7
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.680
It's fully integrated with IAM for authorization,

8
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.880
and it has seamless integration

9
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.920
into all the AWS services pretty much,

10
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.920
so EBS, S3, Redshift, RDS, SSM, et cetera, et cetera.

11
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.870
You should know and remember that KMS keys are regional

12
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.807
and they can only be used in the region they're created in,

13
00:00:30.807 --> 00:00:32.970
and that can come up in the exam a lot.

14
00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.190
So in KMS you have two types of key.

15
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.310
You have the symmetric keys and the asymmetric keys.

16
00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.100
So the symmetric keys were the first offering of KMS.

17
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.320
It's a single encryption key that will be used

18
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.480
to encrypt and decrypt information.

19
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.610
And then all the services that are integrated

20
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.420
with AWS KMS use symmetric KMS keys.

21
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.030
It's necessary if you wanna do envelope encryption,

22
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.740
and you never actually get access

23
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.170
to the KMS key itself or unencrypted.

24
00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.780
You must just send data into KMS

25
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.390
with a KMS API call to use it.

26
00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.670
Now, the asymmetric key are new,

27
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:09.060
and they can allow you to have two kind of public keys,

28
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.360
it's a key pair, so it's a public key and a private key.

29
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.400
The public key will be used to encrypt,

30
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.650
and the private key will be used to decrypt.

31
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.480
Now, this is very helpful when you want to have

32
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.700
encrypt/decrypt or sign/verify operations,

33
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.140
and you can download the public key.

34
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.450
That means that you can encrypt stuff

35
00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.310
from anywhere with a public key,

36
00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.560
and you can send the public key to people

37
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.840
that you don't trust in untrusted environments.

38
00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.990
But once the data is encrypted with the public key,

39
00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.580
only the private key has the power

40
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.410
to decrypt your information.

41
00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.480
You still cannot use the private key unencrypted though.

42
00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.090
You have to use the KMS API calls.

43
00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.180
So the use case for an asymmetric key

44
00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.820
is to have encryptions outside of AWS by users

45
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.230
who cannot directly call the KMS API for whatever reason.

46
00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.660
Now, you have different types of KMS keys.

47
00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.510
The first type is a customer-managed key,

48
00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.580
and this is a key you create directly in KMS.

49
00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.410
So you can create, manage, and use them.

50
00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.120
You can enable, disable them.

51
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.890
You can enable a rotation policy

52
00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.840
to rotate the key every year

53
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.060
while the old key is still preserved, of course.

54
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.640
And you can add a key policy, which is a resource policy

55
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.420
for KMS keys, and you can audit the key usage in CloudTrail.

56
00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.710
This is the kind of keys you will leverage

57
00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.750
for envelope encryption, so these are the kind of keys

58
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.190
that you manage yourself.

59
00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.400
Therefore, they're called customer-managed keys.

60
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.910
But a second kind of key is the AWS-managed keys.

61
00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:30.510
So these ones are used exclusively by AWS services,

62
00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:35.130
such as aws/s3, aws/ebs, and so on.

63
00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.363
And this is what you see in the UI

64
00:02:36.363 --> 00:02:40.110
when you use the AWS-managed keys.

65
00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.760
So they are managed by AWS

66
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.280
and they're automatically rotated every year.

67
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.830
You can view the key policy and you can audit in CloudTrail,

68
00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.300
but you cannot leverage them

69
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.760
for your own encryption operations.

70
00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.580
And finally, you have AWS-owned keys,

71
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.310
and these are created and managed by AWS,

72
00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.070
used by some services to protect your resources,

73
00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.590
and they can be used across multiple AWS accounts,

74
00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.000
but they're not in your accounts.

75
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.580
They're used by AWS internally,

76
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.460
and you cannot view, use, track or audit these keys.

77
00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.820
But AWS tells you that these keys actually exist.

78
00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.400
Okay, so now if we want to have a summary

79
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.410
of the different types of KMS keys,

80
00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.630
for the customer-managed keys, you can manage metadata,

81
00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.430
you can view metadata,

82
00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:21.263
and you can manage it,

83
00:03:21.263 --> 00:03:23.070
and you can also use for your accounts,

84
00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:26.520
and you can have an automatic rotation every one year.

85
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.090
The AWS managed key can be used in your accounts, okay?

86
00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.380
But you cannot manage them,

87
00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.780
and they're required to be rotated every one year.

88
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:34.650
This is automatic.

89
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.780
And then the owned keys, you just know they exist,

90
00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.030
but you cannot use them or see them.

91
00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:39.863
Okay.

92
00:03:39.863 --> 00:03:41.670
Now, how do you create a KMS key?

93
00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:45.120
So you have what's called a key material origin,

94
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.710
and it cannot be changed after creation,

95
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:48.840
so you have to define it as creation time.

96
00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.920
So you have the KMS, which is the AWS_KMS key material,

97
00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.710
which means that you are going to have KMS

98
00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.860
automatically create, generate, and manage the key

99
00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.210
in its own key store

100
00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.890
to see what happens when you go into KMS

101
00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.540
and start to create a key.

102
00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.210
But you have a second option called external.

103
00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.170
In that case,

104
00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:11.010
while you import the key material directly into the KMS key

105
00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.960
and you are responsible for securing

106
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.960
and managing this key material outside of AWS, okay?

107
00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.940
So maybe you just wanna create it outside

108
00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.500
and then import it into this KMS

109
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.450
and then be done and delete it on your end,

110
00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.000
or you just also wanna keep a copy in KMS,

111
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.560
and therefore you're responsible, obviously,

112
00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.020
for the copy outside of KMS.

113
00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.250
Now, the last option

114
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:32.670
is to use a custom key store called AWS_CLOUDHSM.

115
00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:34.770
And this allows you to create the key material

116
00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:37.320
directly within your HSM cluster

117
00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.990
and manage the key material there.

118
00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.730
So let's consider how it works

119
00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:42.870
when you have a custom key store

120
00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.610
which is backed by CloudHSM.

121
00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.160
So you create your CloudHSM cluster as a custom key store,

122
00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.530
and there is a integration with KMS.

123
00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.810
That means that when KMS creates key,

124
00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.490
well, the key materials are going to be stored

125
00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:55.950
in your HSM cluster that you own and manage.

126
00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.950
That means that the keys live within your HSM cluster.

127
00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:02.370
They are only managed by KMS and used by KMS,

128
00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.500
and all the cryptographic operations

129
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.290
will be directly performed in the HSMs, okay?

130
00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:08.820
So this is the architecture.

131
00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.280
You have your CloudHSM cluster with two AZs,

132
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.280
and it is directly connected to KMS.

133
00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.250
So once you have at least two HSMs for high availability,

134
00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:18.600
then you can integrate with KMS,

135
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.480
and then the users can still use the KMS API

136
00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.370
to view, create, and manage keys.

137
00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.200
But behind the scenes, you know for sure

138
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.260
that KMS will be using your CloudHSM cluster

139
00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.850
for all the cryptographic operations

140
00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.950
and for storing and retrieving these keys.

141
00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:32.850
So why we do this?

142
00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.530
Well, the use case is that you need

143
00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.570
to have direct control over the HSMs

144
00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:40.230
for higher security or for security requirements at least,

145
00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:41.370
or you have a requirement

146
00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:45.960
to store the KMS keys in a dedicated HSM environment.

147
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.450
Now, if you choose to have an external KMS key source,

148
00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.420
then you can bring your own key using your own key material,

149
00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.040
and you are really going to be responsible

150
00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.750
for the key material's security,

151
00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.630
availability, and durability outside of AWS.

152
00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:02.100
There is support for both symmetric and asymmetric KMS keys,

153
00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.050
and it cannot be used with custom key store.

154
00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:05.940
It has to be used on its own.

155
00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:09.240
For this, if you want to have KMS key rotation, of course,

156
00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.700
because the key source is external,

157
00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.440
then you have to rotate it on your own.

158
00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.870
So automatic rotation is not supported.

159
00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:16.703
So how does that work?

160
00:06:16.703 --> 00:06:19.680
Well, the user will create a KMS key in KMS

161
00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.020
and say that it's a symmetric type of key,

162
00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.540
and that the source is external.

163
00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:26.130
Now, the key envelope is created,

164
00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.630
but the key material is not.

165
00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:31.740
So then you download the public key and an import token,

166
00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:35.550
and you're going to use the public key with your internal,

167
00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.280
okay, your externally generated key material

168
00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.700
to create an encryption thanks to the public key.

169
00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.350
Now, this public key,

170
00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:45.390
this private key that has been encrypted

171
00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.730
can be sent back to the KMS service

172
00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.710
using also the import token.

173
00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.920
And then KMS will use some decryption mechanism

174
00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.270
to get the key material

175
00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.600
and store it in your KMS key in there.

176
00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.600
So this is the process to import external keys in KMS, okay?

177
00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.550
Lastly, so you need to know about KMS multi-region key,

178
00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.680
so it's possible for you to create a key,

179
00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.210
for example, in us-east-1

180
00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.540
and have it replicated across multiple regions.

181
00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.850
So they will have the same key material

182
00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.130
and actually the same key ID,

183
00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:19.680
but they will be in different regions.

184
00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:21.060
So the use cases are amazing

185
00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:22.980
because thanks to this, well,

186
00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:25.050
you have the same key in multiple regions,

187
00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:26.850
and that means that you can encrypt in one region

188
00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:30.660
and decrypt in the other regions using the same KMS key ID.

189
00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.340
So you don't need to re-encrypt data

190
00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:34.590
or make cross-region API calls.

191
00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.620
So they will have the same key ID, the same key material,

192
00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.660
the same automatic rotation, and so on.

193
00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:41.880
But these keys are not global keys, okay?

194
00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.190
It's this principle that there is a primary key

195
00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.040
in one region, and all the others are replicas.

196
00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:49.440
Each key can be managed independently,

197
00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.680
and although you can only have one primary key at a time,

198
00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.060
it's possible for you

199
00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.450
to promote one replica into their own primary key.

200
00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:59.520
So the use cases for multi-region keys

201
00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.400
is going to be disaster recovery, global data management,

202
00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.530
for example, for DynamoDB global tables,

203
00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.050
active-active application that span multiple region,

204
00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:10.260
or distributing signing applications, and so on.

205
00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:11.580
So that's it for this lecture.

206
00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.580
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

